# Patent Disclosure

## Title

**User Equipment Method and Apparatus for Transmitting and Receiving Signals Based on Reference-Signal Configuration Information**

## Technical Field

The present disclosure relates generally to wireless communications and, more particularly, to techniques by which a user equipment (UE) transmits and/or receives wireless signals based on reference-signal configuration information.

## Background

A wireless communication system may employ one or more reference signals to support channel estimation, beam management, synchronization, mobility, positioning, interference measurement, link adaptation, and demodulation. Examples include channel-state-information reference signals (CSI-RSs), sounding reference signals (SRSs), demodulation reference signals (DMRSs), phase-tracking reference signals (PTRSs), positioning reference signals, synchronization signals, and other downlink, uplink, or sidelink reference signals.

Reference-signal resources may be configured semi-statically through higher-layer signaling and may be activated, deactivated, selected, or modified through dynamic signaling. As wireless systems support multiple transmission and reception points, bandwidth parts, component carriers, beams, numerologies, and duplexing arrangements, ambiguity may arise regarding which reference-signal configuration applies to a particular transmission or reception occasion. Such ambiguity may result in incorrect beam selection, invalid resource mapping, unnecessary measurements, or collisions with other signals.

Accordingly, techniques are needed for determining transmission and reception parameters from reference-signal configuration information and for handling conflicts, unavailable resources, configuration changes, and missing or inconsistent signaling.

## Summary

In one aspect, a method performed by a UE includes receiving reference-signal configuration information from a network node. The reference-signal configuration information identifies or otherwise indicates one or more reference-signal resources, resource sets, spatial parameters, timing parameters, frequency parameters, sequence parameters, power parameters, quasi-co-location parameters, or associations with one or more data or control signals.

The UE determines, based on the reference-signal configuration information, at least one operational parameter for transmitting or receiving a signal. The operational parameter may include a time-frequency resource, a bandwidth part, a component carrier, a transmission configuration indicator state, a quasi-co-location assumption, a spatial-domain transmission filter, a spatial-domain reception filter, a precoding parameter, a power-control parameter, a timing parameter, a frequency-hopping parameter, a repetition parameter, or a reference-signal association.

The UE transmits or receives the signal in accordance with the determined operational parameter. In some implementations, the UE performs one or more measurements on a configured reference signal and selects or updates the operational parameter based on a measurement result. In some implementations, the UE reports the measurement result or an indication of a selected reference-signal resource to the network node.

The disclosed techniques may improve signaling reliability, beam alignment, resource utilization, and configuration consistency while reducing control-signaling overhead.

