- **FIG. 7** illustrates example components of a UE and a network node.

## Detailed Description

### 1. Wireless Communication Environment

A wireless communication system includes a UE and one or more network nodes. A network node may include a base station, distributed unit, central unit, transmission and reception point, relay node, access point, or another entity capable of communicating with the UE.

The UE may communicate using one or more serving cells, component carriers, bandwidth parts, frequency bands, numerologies, transmission and reception points, antenna panels, or beams. Although certain examples are described with reference to cellular communications, the disclosed techniques may also apply to sidelink, device-to-device, non-terrestrial, integrated access and backhaul, or other wireless communications.

### 2. Reference-Signal Configuration Information

The UE receives reference-signal configuration information through one or more of:

1. system information;
2. radio resource control signaling;
3. a medium-access-control control element;
4. downlink control information;
5. sidelink control information;
6. a predefined rule or stored configuration; or
7. any combination thereof.

The reference-signal configuration information may configure one or more reference-signal resources or resource sets. For a particular resource or resource set, the information may indicate one or more of:

- a reference-signal type;
- a resource identifier or resource-set identifier;
- a serving-cell, carrier, or bandwidth-part identifier;
- a slot, symbol, subframe, frame, or periodicity;
- an offset relative to a timing reference;
- a resource-block allocation or frequency-domain density;
- a comb, cyclic shift, code-domain resource, or orthogonal cover code;
- a sequence identifier, scrambling identifier, or initialization value;
- a number or location of antenna ports;
- a repetition, burst, or hopping pattern;
- a transmission power, power offset, or power-control state;
- a spatial relation;
- a quasi-co-location relationship;
- a transmission configuration indicator (TCI) state;
- an association with a synchronization signal block or another reference signal;
- an association with a physical downlink or uplink channel;
- an activation state or validity interval;
- a priority value;
- a measurement quantity or reporting criterion; or
- a transmission and reception point or beam identifier.

A configuration may be persistent, periodic, semi-persistent, or aperiodic. Higher-layer signaling may establish a set of candidate configurations, and dynamic signaling may activate or select one or more candidates.

### 3. Configuration Determination by the UE

After receiving the reference-signal configuration information, the UE determines whether the information is applicable to a scheduled or configured signal. Applicability may be determined according to one or more contextual attributes, including:

- a cell or carrier on which the signal is transmitted;
- an active bandwidth part;
- a control-resource set or search space associated with scheduling information;
- a transmission and reception point associated with the signal;
- a hybrid automatic repeat request process;
- a channel or signal type;
- a timing relationship between configuration signaling and the signal;
- a UE capability;
- a duplexing direction; or
- a priority or validity indication.

The UE may derive an operational parameter directly from an explicitly indicated field. Alternatively, the UE may derive the parameter implicitly, such as by applying an association between a reference-signal resource and a TCI state, spatial relation, antenna panel, power-control state, or physical channel.

For example, a CSI-RS resource may provide a quasi-co-location source for reception of a physical downlink shared channel. An SRS resource may provide a spatial relation or precoding basis for transmission of a physical uplink shared channel. A DMRS configuration may indicate resource elements and sequence parameters used to demodulate a corresponding data or control channel.

### 4. Receiving a Signal

In a downlink implementation, the UE receives configuration information identifying a reference-signal resource or state associated with a downlink signal. The UE determines a reception parameter based on the identified resource or state.

The reception parameter may include:

- an expected reference-signal location;
- a DMRS port or port group;
- a scrambling or sequence initialization parameter;
- a quasi-co-location assumption;
- a Doppler, delay-spread, average-delay, or spatial-reception assumption;
- a receive beam or spatial-domain reception filter;
- an active antenna panel;
- a channel-estimation procedure; or
- an interference-estimation resource.

The UE monitors the indicated resources and receives the downlink signal using the determined reception parameter. The downlink signal may include a physical downlink control channel, physical downlink shared channel, synchronization signal, broadcast signal, paging signal, multicast signal, or another downlink transmission.

In one example, the UE receives an indication of a first TCI state that identifies a first CSI-RS resource. The UE determines a spatial-domain reception filter based on measurements of the first CSI-RS resource and applies the filter to receive a scheduled downlink channel.

If the first CSI-RS resource is unavailable, outside a validity period, or inconsistent with the scheduled transmission, the UE may apply a fallback reception assumption. The fallback assumption may be associated with a synchronization signal block, a serving-beam reference signal, a lowest-indexed valid resource, a most recently activated state, or a predefined default state.

### 5. Transmitting a Signal

In an uplink implementation, the UE receives configuration information identifying an uplink reference-signal resource or a downlink reference signal having a configured spatial relationship with an uplink transmission. The UE determines a transmission parameter based on the identified reference signal.

The transmission parameter may include:

- an uplink transmit beam or spatial-domain transmission filter;
- a precoder;
- an antenna port or panel;
- a path-loss reference;
- a power-control state;
- an open-loop or closed-loop power-control parameter;
- a timing advance;
- a frequency-hopping pattern;
- a sequence parameter;
- a cyclic shift;
- a repetition pattern; or
- a time-frequency resource.

The UE transmits an uplink signal using the determined transmission parameter. The uplink signal may include an SRS, random-access signal, physical uplink control channel, physical uplink shared channel, positioning signal, or another uplink transmission.

In one example, the UE receives an SRS resource-set configuration and an activation command identifying a selected SRS resource. The UE transmits the selected SRS using a spatial-domain transmission filter corresponding to a configured spatial-relation reference. The UE may subsequently use a precoder or beam derived from the selected SRS resource to transmit an uplink data channel.

### 6. Measurement-Based Selection

The reference-signal configuration information may identify a plurality of candidate resources. The UE performs measurements on at least a subset of the candidate resources and selects a resource according to a selection criterion.

A measurement may include reference-signal received power, reference-signal received quality, signal-to-interference-plus-noise ratio, channel-state information, delay, angle, Doppler, path loss, blockage state, or another channel metric.

The selection criterion may include:

- a highest or threshold-satisfying measurement value;
- a resource corresponding to a preferred beam;
- a resource associated with a permitted carrier or bandwidth part;
- a resource satisfying latency or reliability requirements;
- a resource having a required quasi-co-location property; or
- a resource selected according to network-indicated priority.

The UE may report the selected resource, one or more measurement values, or a ranked list of resources. The network node may then dynamically indicate a resource or operational state for a subsequent transmission or reception.

### 7. Activation and Application Timing

A reference-signal configuration may become effective at an application time determined from:

- receipt or successful decoding of configuration signaling;
- transmission of an acknowledgment;
- expiration of a processing-time interval;
- a slot offset indicated by the network;
- activation of a bandwidth part or serving cell;
- receipt of a MAC control element or downlink control indication; or
- occurrence of a configured reference-signal occasion.

The UE may continue using a previous valid configuration until the application time. After the application time, the UE applies the newly activated configuration to signals associated with the configuration.

Where a configuration is deactivated, the UE may release the corresponding operational state, cease measurements, stop transmitting the associated reference signal, or retain the configuration for a defined retention interval.

### 8. Conflict Resolution and Error Handling

Two or more reference-signal configurations may overlap or provide inconsistent operational parameters. The UE may resolve such a conflict using a predetermined priority order.

For example, the UE may prioritize:

1. dynamic signaling over semi-static signaling;
2. dedicated signaling over common signaling;
3. a later-received valid command over an earlier command;
4. a configuration associated with the scheduled channel over a general configuration;
5. a higher-priority service or transmission;
6. a resource having a lower resource identifier; or
7. a resource that avoids a half-duplex or collision constraint.

When a configured reference-signal resource collides with a higher-priority signal, the UE may omit, puncture, postpone, or partially transmit the reference signal. For reception, the UE may omit measurement samples affected by the collision or use a fallback resource.

If the UE cannot determine a valid operational parameter, the UE may refrain from transmission, monitor using a default reception state, transmit an error indication, request reconfiguration, or apply a predefined configuration.

### 9. Example UE Procedure

A method performed by a UE may include:

1. receiving, from a network node, reference-signal configuration information indicating a plurality of reference-signal resources;
2. receiving activation information selecting at least one of the plurality of reference-signal resources;
3. determining that the selected reference-signal resource is associated with a physical channel;
4. determining, from the selected reference-signal resource, a spatial parameter and at least one time-frequency or sequence parameter;
5. transmitting or receiving the physical channel using the spatial parameter; and
6. transmitting or receiving a reference signal using the time-frequency or sequence parameter.

The method may further include measuring one or more candidate reference-signal resources, selecting a preferred resource based on the measurements, and reporting the preferred resource to the network node.

### 10. UE and Network Apparatus

A UE may include one or more transceivers, one or more processors, and memory storing instructions. When executed by the one or more processors, the instructions cause the UE to:

- receive reference-signal configuration information;
- determine an applicable reference-signal resource or state;
- derive a transmission or reception parameter from the resource or state; and
- transmit or receive a signal according to the derived parameter.

A network node may include one or more transceivers, processors, and memory configured to generate and transmit the reference-signal configuration information, receive a measurement report or uplink signal, and schedule a subsequent signal based on the configured reference-signal resource.

The functionality may be implemented in hardware, software, firmware, or any combination thereof. Instructions may be stored on a non-transitory computer-readable medium.

## Example Claims

### Claim 1 — UE Method

1. A method performed by a user equipment, the method comprising:  
   receiving, from a network node, reference-signal configuration information identifying at least one reference-signal resource;  
   determining, based on the reference-signal configuration information, an operational parameter associated with a signal, the operational parameter comprising at least one of a time-frequency parameter, a sequence parameter, a power-control parameter, a quasi-co-location parameter, or a spatial-domain filter; and  
   transmitting or receiving the signal in accordance with the operational parameter.

### Claim 2

2. The method of claim 1, wherein the reference-signal configuration information identifies a plurality of candidate reference-signal resources, and wherein the method further comprises selecting the at least one reference-signal resource from the plurality of candidate reference-signal resources based on one or more measurements.

### Claim 3

3. The method of claim 2, further comprising transmitting, to the network node, a report identifying the selected reference-signal resource or indicating a measurement associated with the selected reference-signal resource.

### Claim 4

4. The method of claim 1, wherein the reference-signal configuration information is received through higher-layer signaling, and wherein the method further comprises receiving dynamic signaling that activates the at least one reference-signal resource.

### Claim 5

5. The method of claim 1, wherein receiving the signal comprises applying a spatial-domain reception filter determined from a quasi-co-location association between the signal and the at least one reference-signal resource.

### Claim 6

6. The method of claim 1, wherein transmitting the signal comprises applying a spatial-domain transmission filter determined from a spatial relationship between an uplink transmission and the at least one reference-signal resource.

### Claim 7

7. The method of claim 1, wherein the signal comprises at least one of a physical downlink shared channel, a physical downlink control channel, a physical uplink shared channel, a physical uplink control channel, a sounding reference signal, or a channel-state-information reference signal.

### Claim 8

8. The method of claim 1, further comprising determining that the at least one reference-signal resource is unavailable or conflicts with another signal and, in response, applying a fallback reference-signal resource or a default operational parameter.

### Claim 9 — UE Apparatus

9. A user equipment comprising:  
   a transceiver; and  
   one or more processors coupled to the transceiver and configured to:  
   receive reference-signal configuration information identifying at least one reference-signal resource;  
   determine, based on the reference-signal configuration information, an operational parameter for a signal; and  
   cause the transceiver to transmit or receive the signal in accordance with the operational parameter.

### Claim 10 — Computer-Readable Medium

10. A non-transitory computer-readable medium storing instructions that, when executed by one or more processors of a user equipment, cause the user equipment to perform the method of any of claims 1–8.

## Abstract

A method, user equipment, and computer-readable medium for wireless communication are disclosed. A user equipment receives reference-signal configuration information identifying one or more reference-signal resources. The user equipment determines an operational parameter for transmitting or receiving a signal based on the reference-signal configuration information. The operational parameter may include a time-frequency resource, sequence parameter, power-control parameter, quasi-co-location assumption, spatial-domain filter, precoder, or reference-signal association. The user equipment transmits or receives the signal in accordance with the determined operational parameter. The user equipment may measure candidate reference-signal resources, select a resource based on the measurements, report the selected resource, apply activation timing, resolve configuration conflicts, or use a fallback resource when a configured resource is unavailable.

### Title  
**Communication Method and System for Converged Fifth-Generation and Internet-of-Things Communications with Configurable Reference-Signal Reception**

### Abstract  
A communication method and system are provided for converging fifth-generation wireless communication technology with Internet-of-Things communication. A network node obtains capability information and/or operating-condition information associated with a terminal and determines, based at least in part on the obtained information, a reference-signal reception configuration for the terminal. The reference-signal reception configuration identifies one or more reference signals and specifies at least one reception parameter, including a time-domain occasion, frequency-domain resource, periodicity, bandwidth, antenna port, beam, sequence, repetition level, measurement behavior, or power-saving condition. The network node transmits configuration information indicating the reference-signal reception configuration. The terminal receives the configuration information and selectively receives or measures the one or more reference signals in accordance with the indicated configuration. The configuration may be adapted according to terminal type, traffic characteristics, mobility, coverage condition, battery state, discontinuous-reception operation, or a quality measurement, thereby supporting reduced-power and coverage-enhanced IoT terminals within a fifth-generation communication system.

### Detailed Description  
In an embodiment, a wireless communication system comprises a network node and a plurality of terminals, at least one of which is an Internet-of-Things terminal. The network node may comprise a fifth-generation base station, a distributed unit, a centralized unit, a radio access network controller, or another network entity configured to control radio-resource use. The terminal may comprise a user equipment, sensor, actuator, meter, wearable device, industrial device, reduced-capability terminal, or machine-type communication device.

The terminal transmits terminal information to the network node. The terminal information may indicate one or more of:

- a terminal category or reduced-capability status;  
- a supported receiving bandwidth or number of receive chains;  
- a supported reference-signal type;  
- a battery or power-saving state;  
- a mobility state;  
- a coverage-enhancement level;  
- a discontinuous-reception configuration;  
- a traffic periodicity or latency requirement;  
- a beamforming or positioning capability; and  
- a measured radio condition.

Based on the terminal information and/or network conditions, the network node generates a reference-signal reception configuration specific to the terminal or to a group of terminals. The reference-signal reception configuration may configure reception of one or more synchronization signals, channel-state-information reference signals, demodulation reference signals, tracking reference signals, positioning reference signals, sounding-related signals, discovery signals, wake-up signals, or other known or subsequently developed reference signals.

The reference-signal reception configuration may specify one or more of:

1. a radio frame, subframe, slot, symbol, or reception window;  
2. a periodic, semi-persistent, aperiodic, or event-triggered reception occasion;  
3. a frequency allocation, bandwidth part, resource block, subcarrier spacing, or carrier;  
4. a reference-signal resource, resource set, sequence, scrambling identifier, or antenna port;  
5. a transmission and reception point, spatial filter, quasi-colocation relationship, or beam identifier;  
6. a repetition number, combining rule, or coverage-enhancement parameter;  
7. a measurement quantity, threshold, averaging parameter, or reporting condition; and  
8. a condition under which reception is activated, suspended, modified, or released.

The network node transmits the configuration information using system information, radio-resource-control signaling, a medium-access-control control element, downlink control information, or a combination thereof. In certain embodiments, a first portion of the configuration is common to a group of IoT terminals and a second portion is dedicated to an individual terminal.

Upon receiving the configuration information, the terminal controls at least one receiver component according to the configured reference-signal reception occasions. The terminal may deactivate a radio-frequency chain, baseband circuit, antenna path, or measurement function outside the configured reception occasions. At a configured occasion, the terminal receives and processes the applicable reference signal to perform synchronization, channel estimation, beam management, mobility evaluation, positioning, radio-link monitoring, or demodulation.

The terminal may transmit a measurement report or reception-status indication to the network node. The network node may modify the reference-signal reception configuration in response to the report, a change in traffic demand, a mobility event, a deterioration in link quality, a battery condition, or a transition between connected, inactive, and idle states. For example, the network node may increase the periodicity interval when the terminal is stationary and has infrequent traffic, and may decrease the periodicity interval or increase repetition when the terminal experiences mobility or poor coverage.

### Exemplary Method Claim  
1. **A communication method performed by a network node of a wireless communication system, the method comprising:**  
   obtaining information associated with a terminal configured for Internet-of-Things communication;  
   determining, based at least in part on the information, a reference-signal reception configuration for the terminal, the reference-signal reception configuration identifying at least one reference signal and at least one reception parameter applicable to reception of the at least one reference signal;  
   transmitting, to the terminal, configuration information indicating the reference-signal reception configuration; and  
   causing the terminal to selectively receive or measure the at least one reference signal according to the reference-signal reception configuration.

2. The method of claim 1, wherein the at least one reception parameter comprises at least one of a reception occasion, periodicity, frequency resource, bandwidth part, antenna port, spatial filter, beam identifier, repetition level, measurement threshold, or activation condition.

3. The method of claim 1, wherein the information associated with the terminal comprises at least one of terminal capability information, a power state, a mobility state, a coverage condition, a traffic characteristic, or a discontinuous-reception parameter.

4. The method of claim 1, further comprising modifying the reference-signal reception configuration based on a measurement report received from the terminal.

5. The method of claim 1, wherein the reference-signal reception configuration causes the terminal to disable at least a portion of a receiver outside one or more configured reception occasions.

### Exemplary Terminal Claim  
6. **A terminal comprising:**  
   at least one transceiver; and  
   at least one processor coupled to the at least one transceiver and configured to:  
   receive, from a network node, configuration information indicating a reference-signal reception configuration, the reference-signal reception configuration being determined based at least in part on an Internet-of-Things operating characteristic of the terminal;  
   identify, from the configuration information, at least one reference signal and at least one reception parameter; and  
   control the at least one transceiver to selectively receive or measure the at least one reference signal according to the at least one reception parameter.

### Exemplary System Claim  
7. **A wireless communication system comprising:**  
   a network node configured to determine and transmit a terminal-specific reference-signal reception configuration based on an operating characteristic of an Internet-of-Things terminal; and  
   the Internet-of-Things terminal configured to receive the terminal-specific reference-signal reception configuration and selectively monitor one or more fifth-generation reference signals in accordance therewith,  
   wherein the terminal-specific reference-signal reception configuration is dynamically adjustable to balance communication reliability, coverage, latency, and terminal power consumption.
